<Docs slug="">
      <p>If you have asked an agent for a test and got back something that looked right but used a button that doesn't exist, or ignored every convention in your project, Cue is built for that problem.</p>
      <h2 id="intro-two">The two things it does</h2>
      <div class="cards">
        <a class="dcard" href={url('docs/rules/')}><strong>Checks your tests</strong><span>Rules that run on every save and every push. A test that breaks one fails the build, not the code review.</span></a>
        <a class="dcard" href={url('docs/survey/')}><strong>Learns your app</strong><span>It visits your app, writes down what is on each page and remembers, so nobody guesses what a button is called.</span></a>
      </div>
      <h2 id="intro-guessing">Why guessing is the expensive part</h2>
      <p>An agent has never seen your app. Ask it for the log-in button and it invents something plausible, like <code>#login-btn</code>. The test then fails as if your app were broken, and somebody spends an afternoon debugging a login page that works.</p>
      <div class="callout note"><strong>Note</strong><p>When something isn't in the map, Cue says "I don't know, go and look" instead of guessing. That refusal is the point.</p></div>
      <h2 id="intro-isnt">What it isn't</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Not a Playwright tutorial.</strong> Playwright's own docs are kept current by the people who build it. Cue points you at them.</li>
        <li><strong>Not a replacement for your process.</strong> It has opinions about tests, none about your roadmap or tickets.</li>
        <li><strong>Not magic.</strong> It stops an agent from inventing details it doesn't have. It can't explain a feature nobody has explained.</li>
      </ul>
    
</Docs>
